'''''Desramé v. Estret Provincial Housing Board''''' (1971–1972) was a landmark {{w|court case}} in [[Balboa]] in which [[Jean-Claude Desramé]], a Black Balboan resident of [[Sant Cristòfor]], challenged the Estret Provincial Housing Board's allocation of public housing units. The [[National Anti-Discrimination Tribunal]] ruled in Desramé's favour, finding that the Housing Board's neighbourhood assignment practices constituted {{w|racial discrimination}} under the [[Twelve Guarantees]]. The ruling established that the Guarantees' protections extended to housing allocation and that {{w|redlining|neighbourhood-based assignment}} practices which produced racial segregation were discriminatory.

== Background ==

Following reunification, the Estret Provincial Housing Board managed the largest public housing portfolio in Balboa, including the estates built during the [[International Mandate of Sant Cristòfor|mandate period]] across the ciutats of [[Sant Cristòfor]] and the newer developments in [[Portnou]]. The Housing Board's allocation policy assigned applicants to housing estates based on a "community fit" assessment, which considered factors including the applicant's existing social networks, language, and "cultural compatibility" with the estate's existing residents.

In practice, the "community fit" assessment produced a pattern in which Black Balboan applicants were assigned to estates in the inland ciutats — predominantly [[Sant Martí (ciutat)|Sant Martí]] and [[Ciutat de l'Estació (ciutat)|Ciutat de l'Estació]] — which were predominantly Black Balboan and had lower density and fewer amenities. White and Creole applicants were assigned to estates in the waterfront ciutats, including [[Costa Nord (ciutat)|Costa Nord]] and [[Mare de Déu del Mar (ciutat)|Mare de Déu del Mar]], which were predominantly white and mixed and had better access to transport, schools, and services. The result was that public housing in Sant Cristòfor was effectively segregated along racial lines, despite the absence of any explicit racial criterion in the allocation policy.

[[Jean-Claude Desramé]] was a Black Balboan hotel employee who applied for public housing in 1970. Desramé requested placement in [[Costa Nord (ciutat)|Costa Nord]], where a new public housing development had recently been completed near the hotel where he worked. The Housing Board assigned him to an estate in [[Sant Martí (ciutat)|Sant Martí]], citing "community fit" and the availability of units. Desramé's white colleague, who had applied at the same time with a similar profile, was assigned to the Costa Nord development.

== Case ==

Desramé brought a case before the [[National Anti-Discrimination Tribunal]] in 1971, arguing that the Housing Board's "community fit" assessment was a proxy for racial {{w|discrimination}} and that the resulting segregation violated the [[Twelve Guarantees]]. Desramé's legal team presented statistical evidence drawn from the Housing Board's own allocation records, demonstrating that Black Balboan applicants were systematically assigned to estates in Sant Martí and Ciutat de l'Estació regardless of their stated preferences, family connections, or workplace location.

The Housing Board defended the "community fit" assessment on the grounds that it was designed to promote social cohesion and reduce tenant turnover by placing applicants in communities where they had existing social networks. The Board argued that the assessment did not consider race and that the resulting patterns reflected self-selection and social networks rather than discrimination.

== Ruling ==

The Tribunal ruled in Desramé's favour in 1972, finding that the "community fit" assessment constituted indirect {{w|racial discrimination}}. The Tribunal held that the assessment, while facially neutral, produced a racially disparate outcome — the effective segregation of public housing — and that the Board's justification of "social cohesion" was insufficient to override the Guarantees' anti-discrimination provisions.

The Tribunal ordered the Housing Board to eliminate the "community fit" assessment and to replace it with an allocation system based on objective criteria including family connections, workplace proximity, and waiting list seniority. The Tribunal also ordered the Board to report its allocation data to the Tribunal annually for a period of five years.

== Significance ==

The Desramé case established that the [[Twelve Guarantees]]' protections extended to housing allocation and that {{w|disparate impact}} — a facially neutral policy producing a racially discriminatory outcome — was actionable under the Guarantees. The ruling built on the precedent established in ''[[Vargas v. Sant Cristòfor Port Authority]]'' (1963) and extended the disparate impact principle from employment to housing.

The ruling had significant practical consequences for public housing in Sant Cristòfor, as the elimination of the "community fit" assessment led to a gradual desegregation of the public housing estates across the city's ciutats over the following decade. The Desramé precedent was later cited in ''[[Sòtol Community Coalition v. Tramuntana Provincial Government]]'' (2017), which extended the disparate impact principle to funding allocation.

